   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Infrastructure</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Across 2024-25, Australia&#x2019;s Western Province Partnership (WPP) funded the Morehead Agriculture and Development Hub infrastructure project. The Hub is expected to unlock the agricultural and economic development of the Morehead region and, from its very beginning, has employed over 50 locals in the construction phase. 
&#x200d;
The Hub was planned and designed through extensive consultation with the Western Provincial Administration, South Fly District Administration, Morehead Local Level Government, as well as the local community. Construction of the Morehead Hub has progressed well and is now over 95% complete, with only minor infrastructure (e.g. guardhouse, haus win, nurseries) and landscaping remaining. 
&#x200d;
This project directly resulted in job-creation for local people, including:
&#x200d;
   -16(M) casual maintenance worker and security guards, engaged by the builder during the main construction phase
&#x200d;
   -10 (4W:6M) labourers and 21 casuals (8W:13M), engaged by WPP to support landscaping and site beautification works
&#x200d;
   -10 (2W:8M) permanent security guards, engaged and trained by WPP&#x2019;s sub contracted accredited local security provider for the Hub.
&#x200d;
WPP is completing further recruitment for a hub manager, farm coordinator, administration officer and driver. Safeguarding for partners was carried out with local casuals, promoting localisation and safer working environment for both women, men and persons with disability.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia continues to strengthen Water, Sanitation and Hygiene (WASH) governance in South Fly District through support to the South Fly District WASH Committee. The South Fly District WASH plan, launched in November 2022, improved the Western Provincial Government&#x2019;s capacity to understand, plan and deliver district WASH services. The District WASH Committee is working closely with the South Fly District Development Authority and Western Provincial Health Authority (WPHA) to track implementation progress and review the district&#x2019;s WASH interventions. The plan also helped the WPHA to justify additional resources, including three short-term positions, to support WASH implementation in South Fly District.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gender equality</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2024-25, Australia&#x2019;s Western Province Partnership (WPP) conducted Organisational Capacity Assessments (OCAs) for three Women&#x2019;s Equality Organisations &#x2013; the Daru Urban, South Fly District, and Western Provincial Councils of Women &#x2013; as well as the South Fly District Disabled Person&#x2019;s Organisation and Daru Market Women&#x2019;s Vendors Association. Informed by the OCA outcomes, WPP supported these groups to develop tailored Capacity Development and Strategic Plans, in parallel with targeted leadership and advocacy training.
&#x200d;
In collaboration with the Provincial Division of Community Development, the Department of Provincial and Local Government Affairs, and the Provincial Assembly Clerk, WPP supported the three Councils of Women to create standard operating procedures consistent with PNG&#x2019;s legislation. These provide a structured framework legitimising and empowering women&#x2019;s representation and participation in the Provincial Executive Council and Local Level Government Assemblies.
&#x200d;
With mentoring and technical assistance from WPP, the Western Provincial Council of Women developed a strategic roadmap to help reposition the organisation at the national level. Delivered in partnership with World Vision, these efforts strengthened the Council&#x2019;s case for representation in the National Council of Women Executive, ultimately resulting in the Western Provincial Council of Women Chairperson&#x2019;s successful election as National Vice-President.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In FY2023/24, the Western Province Partnership (WPP) program worked with the Western Provincial Administration (WPA) to assess capacity and barriers to meeting its service delivery commitments. The OCA identified planning, public financial management, human resource management and staff capacity as key systemic challenges. The WPA, with WPP&apos;s technical advisory support, established a senior level working group to develop a Provincial Capacity Development Plan (PCDP) to address agreed service delivery and capacity issues identified by the OCA. WPP is providing further support for PCDP implementation. Informed by the OCA/PCDP, the WPA adjusted upcoming recruitment to prioritise key capacity/skills gaps. WPP embedded its Monitoring and Evaluation Manager part-time with the WPA to address its low level of planning capacity and support provincial planning staff to develop the new 5-Year Provincial Development Plan (2023-27) for submission to National Government for endorsement.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">South Fly District in PNG&#x2019;s Western Province is geographically remote and vulnerable to natural disasters and the impacts of climate change. Most communities do not have access to town water or power. Australia&#x2019;s Western Province Partnership (WPP), in partnership with World Vision, continues to deliver critical water infrastructure and strengthen water security, sanitation and hygiene in communities across South Fly District. 
&#x200d;
In 2024-25, WPP delivered vital water infrastructure and WASH activities, aligned with the District 5-Year WASH Plan and informed by climate risk considerations, to maximise sustainability and resilience against future environmental challenges:
&#x200d;
   -24 boreholes with 16 handpumps installed and maintained  
&#x200d;
   -4 community halls, equipped with rainwater catchment structures and tanks
&#x200d;
   -25 rainwater catchment structures and tanks rehabilitated 
&#x200d;
   -24 Community WASH Committees established and trained.
&#x200d;
In total, 14,148 people (7,308M:6,840W), including 195 (107M:88W) people living with disability, benefited from improved access to basic drinking water services.
&#x200d;
In recognition of the health risk of waterborne diseases and poor sanitation, WPP also funded World Vision to support 54 communities in Community Led Total Sanitation and install 776 latrines. 14 communities were declared Open Defecation Free (ODF) and 3 self-declared ODF communities are awaiting formal certification from the Western Provincial Health Authority.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">South Fly Resilience Plan (SFRP) working with PNG National Agriculture Research Institute (NARI) and Department of Agriculture are improving farm practices through the Kibuli model agricultural hub. Fifty-six community leaders (27M:29 W) and 27 community rangers, were trained as farmers/trainers in improved garden practices, horticulture, and nursery management. This improved access and availability to nutritious food to the surrounding 10 villages. Through the SFRP and NARI cultivar trials and trainings a total of 5,951 improved cultivars of taro, cassava, rice seeds, corn, peanut seeds, and spring onion cuttings were distributed to 1,159 farmers.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Australia-Papua New Guinea Western Province Partnership (WPP) is focused on strengthening subnational governance and government service delivery, as well as improving local communities&#x2019; access to economic livelihood opportunities, food and water security. In partnership with World Vision, the WPP has improved access to clean and safe water for communities in the South Fly District of Western Province in Papua New Guinea. Considering factors like proximity, sustainability, risks and safeguards, the WPP commissioned hydrogeological surveys to identify suitable areas with groundwater for borehole drilling, and/or recommended other more appropriate interventions. 

Together, the WPP and World Vision established and maintained critical water infrastructure, reaching 5,695 people in 12 communities, including 2,882 women and 16 people with disabilities. Fourteen boreholes with pumps were drilled and installed, seven water security units were constructed and installed to provide a total capacity of 63,000 litres, and five existing water sources and 12 existing rainwater tanks were rehabilitated. Households in these communities can now access sustainable drinking water sources year-round, making them more resilient to the effects of climate change including extreme drought, flooding, sea-level rise and groundwater salinity</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Australian-funded South Fly Shipping Service (SFSS) addresses a lack of reliable marine transport to Western Province, supporting movement of non-commercial goods between Port Moresby and Daru. SFSS strengthened supply chain resilience by maintaining a reliable open line of transport to Western Province during COVID-19. Since then, SFSS enabled continued delivery of Australian development assistance across various sectors (e.g. health, infrastructure). SFSS also supported the PNG Government (e.g., PNG Electoral Commission, Western Provincial Health Authority) and approved NGO development partners (e.g., Ok Tedi Development Foundation) operating in Western, enabling continued delivery of their respective services and development programs.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
